On Wed, Oct 02, 2002 at 10:59:23AM -0400, Jeff Downs wrote:
>
> I'm using ARC and MILO to boot currently, and would like to keep it that 
> way (for the same reasons you list - flaky SRM).

Can anyone remember:

1. what is the symptom of the "flaky" SRM?
2. is there a fix?

I know that the patch for the correct resumption from a trap, which
problem afflicts SRM consoles for NONAME/MULTIA/JENSEN, was indeed
fixed in the 7.2 kernel, because I tested for it on JENSEN.

> If anyone has upgraded to 7.2 with MILO successfully, please share.
> The install docs for 7.2 seem to indicate that it does work, but is 
> unsupported.

I did installs of 7.2 using MILO on XLT-366 which went fine, and my
experiences were probably used as the basis for the docs... ;-}

As the docs say, you MUST be using a reasonably new MILO in order to
handle the default "sparse superblock" setup of large partitions with
EXT2, as well as supply the *required* INITRD functionality. If you
are using an older MILO, it all *may* work if you use a RAMdisk load
technique from floppy, but I don't know for sure, I never tried that.

In addition, I did actually attempt an install on MULTIA under SRM with
floppy, which, IIRC, did start up hopefully, but at some point crashed,
again IIRC, with signal 11 or some such, which may be the "flaky SRM"
problem rearing its ugly head.... :-\

Good luck.

 --Jay++

