On Wed, Oct 02, 2002 at 11:34:58AM -0400, Jay Estabrook wrote:
> On Wed, Oct 02, 2002 at 10:59:23AM -0400, Jeff Downs wrote:
> 
> > If anyone has upgraded to 7.2 with MILO successfully, please share.
> > The install docs for 7.2 seem to indicate that it does work, but is 
> > unsupported.
> 
> I did installs of 7.2 using MILO on XLT-366 which went fine, and my
> experiences were probably used as the basis for the docs... ;-}

I also installed a while ago 7.2 on a machine with MILO (I already
forgot what was that precisely; not MULTIA and SRM just does not exist
for that board) without anything unusual.  Just worked. But I used a
fairly new MILO from Stefan Reinauer web page while doing that.

> As the docs say, you MUST be using a reasonably new MILO in order to
> handle the default "sparse superblock" setup of large partitions with
> EXT2,

That is easy to circumvent.  Just a use a small ext2 partition to be
mounted on /boot.  MILO does not need to see anything else.  You can
always run mke2fs yourself on it with '-b 1024 -O none' to be sure and
no "sparse superblock" will show up.  For other partitions once you
loaded your kernel any partition type which can be handled by it is a
game.

The more serious is that default distro kernels are compiled with
LEGACY_LOAD_ADDRESS (option name?) not set in their configurations and
you either need a MILO which can handle that or you have to recompile
yourself a custom kernel both for booting (replace the one on a floppy)
and for running.

   Michal




