I suppose that the restrictions have not been lifted ( ie distribution of
binary renditions of the JDK/SDK/JRE to the general public ). I suppose
that the "tell me what you did to make it work" clause is still there. I
suppose that the time limit of your license is still one year.

There is no JIT, or hotjava. Compaq has a JIT, bit has not released the
source ( as far as i know ).
Not even sure that there is the call-native ( an asm piece ) written  for
the alpha. A real bummer if you dont understand the original x86 assembler
code in order to translate into corresponding alpha code

But in all, the standard start  is to obtain the .class files and place
them where the makefile scripts checks out the date stamps. The idea is to
have the date on the .class files to be greater than the date on the
corresponding java files. Then the scripts will create the "C" objects, and
not bother with the .class files.
The .class files can be taken from "ANY" build ( solaris, x86 ... ) . The
'native' calls wasn't much of an issue ( except for the small assembler
interface between java & 'C' ) till much later in the build.

I dont know how 64bit clean is the product, but it wasnt too clean, but not
all that dirty. Maybe the "Compaq/Java" port patches have been incorporated
into the release, but from my past experience ( as well as that of some
compaq employee's ) thats not likely.
/gat

BTW the berkely unix for alpha group wanted to build an alpha/java port (
they finally got a binary license ) . I dont know how far they got with the
project.



