>
> Another work around for lazy coding in 99.9% of the cases. Bugs like this that SEGV but magically disappear when "-mieee" is used are almost always a case of an uninitialized floating point variable that contains leftover stack garbage on first use.

It may be that many of the blowups -mieee "solves" are of this
type.  However, I have seen cases in the rainbow text indexing
and classification package and then in small test cases where
perfectly legal and fully initialized operations give floating
point exceptions if -mieee is not used and work correctly (within
the limits of numeric precision and range) with it.

If I remember correctly, the case involved taking effectively
log(exp(-610)) or exp(log(1e-610)) or something like that.  (No,
it did not involve taking a log() of a negative number.)  Without
the -mieee switch, the first operation produced a NAN, and the
second operation threw an exception.  With the switch, the test
case does what one would expect, with no exception.

This would appear to be one of the 0.1% of the cases (by your
estimation) where the switch is a workaround rather than
necessary to get correct execution of a correct program.

Robert Riches
