> On Tuesday 08 October 2002 23:01, Alvin Starr wrote:
> 
>>Alvin Starr wrote:
>>
>>>I got gcc 3.2 up and running on my system and have been able to rebuild
>>>a kernel with it but have not yet had a chance to test it.
>>>
>>>Although I have been seeing ifconfig crash on a couple of our systems.
>>>it occured to me that the problem may be related to the kernel problem
>>>so I did a simple compile with the "-O2" removed(no optimization) and
>>>amazingly the ifconfig crash went away. This was with the old 2.96.xxx
>>>compiler. I then tried to build ifconfig with my new gcc 3.2 and -O2. No
>>>luck ifconfig still crashes. I then tried to build it again with my
>>>2.96.xxx and using "-O2 -fno-strength-reduce" and low and behold. Things
>>>work again.
>>
>>Oops. the -fno-strength-reduce did not actually make the problem go away
>>  but the  removing of the -O2 did.
> 
> 
> try, -fno-sched-interblock

still segfaults. With both 2.96 and 3.2.

trying to work with gdb on a optimized program can be a bit of a pain.
My best guess now is that the stack is getting clobbered by a buffer 
overflow that not caught by an unoptimized program because ther are some 
dead variables that are left hanging around.




