What you see is still an illusion. The FP unit, handles a limited range of float operations. Anything outside of the 'norm' is passed to the OS as a FP exception by the FP hardware. The kernel then evaluates the failed instruction to see if it follows
the alpha/ieee/cc -mieee convention(s), or is just optimized for FP efficiency.  If u use the -mieee switch the compiler emits (more) code that the kernel will recognize that it is being asked to substitute the lack of ieee hardware by kernel software.
for example, any de-normal number will cause a FP-hardware fault ( -mieee or not enabled ) which will throw a FP exception towards the kernel. With -mieee, the kernel recognizes that it is being asked to fix the fault by hand. Without -mieee you just get
the fault, because the kernel does not know how to 'backtrack' to the exact instruction in the instruction pipe-line.



