I suppose one might say that all misalignments are trapped. The kernel then
figures out what to do.
If the misalignment is within the kernel, that also traps, but the kernel gets
a little peaved. This generally happens when a new device driver is included (
ie never tested on a 64bit machine before.)
If misalignment is within user space, then there is a per-process flag ( i
think a dec-unix ( OSF?) flag ) to indicate that a user fault should happen, or
an automatic 'fixup' should happen ( and the user would be none to the
wiser.)   I think with the 'user fault' category, then kernel can either stop
the task, or trap to the user task for the user to determine what to do.

And Yes, gcc et al ( particularuly ) the runtime start-up code does set this
flag. I cannot tell you who deceides this ( ie kernel is set to trap, but glibc
tells the kernel to silently fix these things up. or the other way around.)

I would like to see the unalignment faults, maybe then these things 'might' get
fixed. The theory, i suppose, is that maybe you can find the 'coder' who in all
likelyhood only thinks in intel, and probably has no time to delve into the
problem - ergo a lot of trap messages with no one to repair same!

/gat

