Your opinion is amusing.  If the kernel didnt bother fixing these faults, then
you'd have a dead program - but some incentive to fix the problem. If the kernel
could fix them, then you pay the price in delayed execution of that faulty
instruction - with fairly no incentive to go and get it fixed. If the processor is
so fast, then maybe its not even worth bothering.
So what would it take for this fault to escalate into a serious matter.  On a
266Mhz 21064 machine, it may warrent an evaluation. on a 800mhz 21264, under the
same condidions it may warrent a yawn.  And a different type of person may change
that concept alltogether.

