On Mon, Oct 14, 2002 at 04:58:28PM +0100, Pedro AF. Cunha wrote:
>  
> I've an Alphaserver 800 5/400 and I'm trying to install, from SRM,
> the Red Hat 7.2.
>  
> After doing all the needed selections, the installation continues
> copying packages and then freezes randomly. It seems to me to be a
> SCSI problem, because after freezing starts doing some SCSI resets.
>  
> This is the list of the Hardware:
> 2 x NCR 53c810 (External storage atached)
> 2 x DE500
> Internal SCSI controller (qlogic ISP1020) with CDROM and two SCSI
> disks connected in the internal inclosure (the Operating System
> disks).
>
> My questions:
> As any one successfully installed this version in this type of
> Alphaserver? Any kind of problems?

Yes, AS800 was one of the platforms we tested during development.

In fact, it was one of the reasons we went to a different QLogic
driver than the one in the stock kernel sources. We encountered many
SCSI-related problems, because the normal AS800 configuration is to
place a SCSI CDROM on the same bus as the harddisks, and the stock
QLogic driver appears extremely sensitive to termination issues with
this setup. We had much better luck with the "feral" driver
(www.feral.com, hi Matt!), so your experience is a bit of a surprise
to me.

Have you tried installing via "text mode" instead of GUI, just in case
the problem is X-related as well?

Also, as Maurice suggested, you could disconnect any external devices
from those NCR810s; when you do, if you connected the CDROM to one of
the 810s, and left the harddisks on the QLogic, it may work better.

Good luck, let me know how you make out.

 --Jay++

