Uncle George wrote:
> alignment faults are guarenteed to generate a trap. Does that necessarily guarentee
> correct results? wello no. You have to teach the kernel, or the user ( if the
> kernel passes the fault to the user ) to process the misalignment.  It may seem
> real simple to fetch 4 bytes from address 6, but you really also have to deceide
> what register the result should be placed in ( u have to decode the faulting
> op-code ) .  Then you have to restore the user process environment, albeit with the
> correct results, and in the proper register.There aren't too many op-codes in a
> risc processor that require memory addressing, so checking is limited .
> 
> My amazment is with 'pipelining' ( multiple instructions in various stages of
> execution ), and insuring that these (fixed) results wont interfere with other
> instructiions in the pipeline. I Think that this is a special case, but I'd have to
> look it up again to be sure.
> /gat

I have spent a good chunk of the last day or so trying to generate a 
case where this problem does actually exist. Sadly I cannot recreate a 
simple bug.  I have a program (l2tpd) that runs a network tunneling 
protocol. Anyway. This program works on streams of bytes that are the 
control packets. Some of the packets have 16bit words that are unaligned 
  when they are going to be written.

So I have a chunk of code like this:

int add_message_type_avp(struct buffer *buf, _u16 type) {
         _u16 *raw = (_u16 *)(buf->start + buf->len);
         raw[0] = htons(0x8 | MBIT);
         raw[1] = htons(VENDOR_ID);
         raw[2] = 0;
         raw[3] = htons(type);
         buf->len += 8;
         return 0;
}

I end up converting it to this:

int add_message_type_avp(struct buffer *buf, _u16 type) {
         _u16 raw[32];
         raw[0] = htons(0x8 | MBIT);
         raw[1] = htons(VENDOR_ID);
         raw[2] = 0;
         raw[3] = htons(type);
         memcpy((char *)(buf->start + buf->len), (char *)&raw,8); 
buf->len += 8;
         return 0;
}


This is not pretty but it does work.

I spent a lot of time tracking this one down and could only come to the 
conclusion that the unaligned trap handler was not working correctly.
I was hoping to come up with a nice small proveable case but I just 
could not seem to make things break on demand. and the only other way to 
get this to break would involve kicking 40 customers off line(Not to 
likely).



