Regardless of all other issues this is a really costly method for
doing this.  You may cause four kernel traps.

> 
> I end up converting it to this:
> 
> int add_message_type_avp(struct buffer *buf, _u16 type) {
>          _u16 raw[32];
>          raw[0] = htons(0x8 | MBIT);
>          raw[1] = htons(VENDOR_ID);
>          raw[2] = 0;
>          raw[3] = htons(type);
>          memcpy((char *)(buf->start + buf->len), (char *)&raw,8); 
> buf->len += 8;
>          return 0;
> }
> 
> 
> This is not pretty but it does work.

Save some code questions this is actually a correct way of coding
something of that sort.  Should compile to something reasonably fast
too.

Here are questions.
  - Why you are pushing on stack raw[32] while only four positions
    are used?
  - Are MBIT and VENDOR_ID really constants as suggested by a case?
    If yes then how about making a static initializer for first three
    positions in raw[4] and filling up only one cell with htons(type)?
  - With a correct size a "magic constant" of 8 can and should be
    replaced by sizeof(raw).

> I spent a lot of time tracking this one down and could only come to the 
> conclusion that the unaligned trap handler was not working correctly.

Seems like there is a potential for few other mess-ups.  You really
eliminated them all?

   Michal




