This has the smell of being possible. I you have a (older) machine that was unable to
fetch bytes/shorts because there was no machine lang instructions to do so. The gcc
compiler would have to generate the appropriate  extract/insert code for each usage of
byte/short.  Not sure how the code would handle the odd address though. But
none-the-less those kernels would only see faults for ints,or longs ( as there were no
short fetches on a machine level )

Now there are machines that can fetch shorts, and bytes on a single instruction level. I
suspect that there wont be an alignment problem with byte, but there is the possibility
that  the alignment fault correction code in the kernel as it relates to the new 'short'
load/store's may not have been extensively tested. A Hypothesis to be sure.

In either case, can u post some of the particulars. ie machine type, gcc version, gcc
compile options?

