On Thursday 17 October 2002 10:47, William H. Magill wrote:
<snip>
>
> ============
> [Note that the compiler referred to above is the Compaq C compiler, NOT
> gcc. Gcc is historically much, much worse with this sort of thing on
> the Alpha. While I would trust CCC on this score, unless GCC has been
> recently (ie in the past year) re-written to address the problems, it
> doesn't deal with Alpha code generation issues well at all.]

Although I also have more faith in CCC.  This is not a compiler issue. I
could hand code alignment failures in assembly.  The question is what
does the kernel do about them.  IIRC, Alvin's premise is that the kernel is 
not fixing up the alignment faults correctly.  I beleive that the kernel
is doing the "right" thing.

>
> The issue of fixing the unaligned access errors is directly related to
> their occurrence. 

I agree.

>One shot programs with single errors are easily
> ignored. However, in a production program even "just a couple" of
> errors can quickly eat up cpu cycles. And in a "common routine" they
> can be disastrous -- much more wall-clock-time is spent not only fixing
> the error, but in writing out the error message than is spent
> generating the results. This yields EXTREMELY poor application
> performance... and it is measured in Wall-clock-time, not in CPU cycles!

yup, IMHO,  it is just a matter of the length of the delay verses the amount of
time that I have as a programmer to find and them.

Best Regards,


--George




