Hi Everyone,
Thank you all for you answers and help.

George, thank you for your code, I actually used the code by Sean Hunter
available at http://linux.iol.unh.edu/linux/alpha/faq/iol_faq.html#9

There was only one instance in our tests that caused unaligned trap. The
runtime code, binary data generated did not produce any unaligned traps.
What we had was:

static uint8_t storage[100000]

testUTrie(){
    utrie_serialize(...,storage,sizeof(storage),..)
}
............ source was passed to a library function that cast the uint8_t
to internal structure ...
struct UTrieHeader {
    uint32_t signature;
    uint32_t options;
    int32_t indexLength;
    int32_t dataLength;
};
utrie_serialize(...., uint8_t* data,int32_t  capacity,....)
UTrieHeader* header = (UTrieHeader*) data;
header->signature =0x54726965; /**  this line caused unaligned trap **/
}

so the fix was to change storage to
struct{
    double bogus; /* needed for aligining the storage */
    uint8_t storage[10000000];
} storageHolder;

The expectation of the API is that input param "data" is aligned.

Some of our tools still cause unaligned traps but that is build time issue
and we decided we can live with the performance degradation.

Regards,

Ram
