> On Thu, Oct 17, 2002 at 03:17:04PM -0700, Ram Viswanadha wrote:
> ....
> >
> > so the fix was to change storage to
> > struct{
> >     double bogus; /* needed for aligining the storage */
> >     uint8_t storage[10000000];
> > } storageHolder;
> >
> > The expectation of the API is that input param "data" is aligned.
>
> Err...  if you are using such tricks to extricate yourself from
> bad "type punning", then I would think that using union instead of
> struct would be vastly preferable.  Not only you avoid reserving
> some memory which we know will be not used for anything but you
> also get much better chances that alignment restriction for the
> widest type will be applied to all union members.
>
> With struct a compiler can insert any amount of padding betwen
> fields.  Sure, in this particular case you would not expect that
> but relying on a compiler "reasonable behaviour" is by its nature
> non C Standard conforming hence not portable.  "Worksforme on x86"
> is behind a huge portion of bugs we are seeing on Alpha (and which
> you will see on Itanium, or Hammer, or ... ).
>
> gcc has a way to affect a stucture layout with '__attribute__ ((packed))'
> but this is "gcc private" construct; widely used in Linux kernel, BTW.
> It does affect what compiler generated to access fields in structures.
>
>   Michal
