On Fri, Oct 18, 2002 at 10:17:55AM -0700, Ram Viswanadha wrote:
> How about the using the below code, we have tested it on all platforms that
> we have ported to and it works, even on platforms like the mainframes.
> 
> /**
>  * This should align the memory properly on any machine.
>  * This is very useful for the safeClone functions.
>  */
> typedef union {
>     long    t1;
>     double  t2;
>     void   *t3;
> } UAlignedMemory;

I would expect this to work in any reasonable situation. I am not
100% sure without study of relevant documents if Standard _guarantees_
what you expect  (anyone can clarify?) but I would be really surprised
if that would fail somewhere.

> 
> /**
>  * Get the amount of bytes that a pointer is off by from
>  * the previous aligned pointer
>  */
> #define U_ALIGNMENT_OFFSET(ptr) (((size_t)ptr) & (sizeof(UAlignedMemory) - 1))

???   Are you relying here that sizeof(UAlignedMemory) is a power of
two?  How you can be sure of that?  This is an implementation detail.

> /**
>  * Get the amount of bytes to add to a pointer
>  * in order to get the next aligned address
>  */
> #define U_ALIGNMENT_OFFSET_UP(ptr) (sizeof(UAlignedMemory) - U_ALIGNMENT_OFFSET(ptr))

I am not sure what all of this is about. If you have
  ptr *UAlignedMemory;
then '&ptr[5];', assuming that you "own" the referenced memory, will
make a compiler to compute properly that address by itself.  The same
if you will use pointers to some of union members.  Second-guessing
pointer arithmetic is often a source of nasty, and non-obvious on the
first look, bugs.  More debugging fun if you will hide behind macros. :-)

   Michal




