Hi Ted,

I recommend you to copy your source tree to make a new kernel with superpage.
It depends on the environment, but following step may work on many redhat.
Assume you have a copy of the patch on my web at home directory.

cd /usr/src
tar cvf - linux-2.4 | (cd ~; tar xf -)
cd
cd linux-2.4
patch -p1 < ../super_page-2.4.9_redhat72_alpha.patch

After that you can configure the kernel as I wrote.
Be sure that you must turn on Super Page Support check box
in the general settings.

Hi Cris, thank you George,

Super Page will extend the virtual to real mapping capability
upto 4MB per TLB entry (8K, 64K, 512K, 4MB will be selected depend on 
the requests of your program). EV5 series processor has 64 entries 
of data tlb, then you can map upto 256MB of virtual address without TLB misses.

Basically multiple processors is not relevant to the Super Page.
The reason why I tested SMP in HP Japan was I must confirm that
it works correctly on SMP. I had some report on SMP but I wanted
to test by myself.

If the dynamic working set of your application is under 512KB,
Super Page will not help you.
The matrix transpose benchmark is one of a ill-behaviour
application for conventional operating systems.
It misses TLB in every two load operations.
If a TLB miss occures, the memory latency will be very large.
The performance improvement on low memory performance processor 
will not show dramatic performance improvement.
I guess that your EV56 may have only about 50 to 100% improvement
on the matrix transpose.

Some Pentium-4 machines marked upto 6 times improvement and
EV6 marked upto 4 times improvement. Then I hope it will
help even EV7 because EV7 may have very good memory performance.


Regards,

